{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q018_tal_effector_repeat_lengths","answer":"The three domains share family a.298.1.1 because SCOPe classifies them by a shared, repeated TAL effector module, not by total protein length. In SCOPe 2.08, d2kq5a_ (PthA, Xanthomonas axonopodis pv. citri), d3v6pa_ (dHax3, Xanthomonas) and d4cj9a_ (BurrH, Burkholderia rhizoxinica) all sit under fold a.298 (left-handed alpha-alpha superhelix), superfamily a.298.1 (TAL effector-like), and family a.298.1.1 (TAL effector). Their lengths differ as the premise says: the genetic SEQRES domain sequences are 51, 373 and 752 residues. The lengths reflect how many repeat units each domain spans, not different classifications.

Why length does not matter here: TAL effector proteins are built from tandem, nearly identical ~34-residue repeats. Most repeat positions are conserved; the hypervariable residues (classically at repeat positions 12 and 13, the RVD) mainly set DNA-base preference. Because the repeat is the recurring, evolutionarily meaningful unit, a domain that contains one repeat (d2kq5a_, annotated 'single repeat') and domains that contain many repeats (d3v6pa_, roughly a dozen repeats; d4cj9a_, a repeat array plus extra non-repeat sequence) can all be the same family. SCOPe records this explicitly: the family carries an 're' inconsistency flag meaning the common domain can be composed of a different number of small repeating units, and the flag is applied to all domains of the family. The repeat annotation uses 4cj9 A:259-292 (about 34 residues) as the canonical representative repeat. Its coordinates apply directly to d4cj9a_ but are not coordinates for the other two domains, so they should not be read as their boundaries.

Limits: (1) Family-level agreement is a curated repeat-architecture homology call at family resolution; it does not show that the three proteins have the same DNA target or the same function. Repeat identity and RVD content determine specificity, and that must come from sequence or experimental evidence, not the SCOPe lineage. (2) The lineage is for the three listed reference domains only; it does not assign classification, boundaries or domain counts to any other TAL-like protein or to the full-length proteins. (3) The Pfam and UniProt cross-reference lists for these domains are empty in this release, so no external family cross-check is available here. (4) Domain length is not a classification criterion, but the three domains are not equivalent units, so a 51-residue single repeat should not be treated as a complete model of the 373- or 752-residue domains.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_domains (2.08; parents and annotations for d2kq5a_, d3v6pa_, d4cj9a_)","mcp__scope__get_scop_node (a.298.1.1, parents)","mcp__scope__get_scop_sequence (genetic SEQRES domain sequences, lengths 51/373/752)","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d2kq5a_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d2kq5a_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3v6pa_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3v6pa_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4cj9a_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4cj9a_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.298.1.1/parents","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d2kq5a_","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d3v6pa_","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d4cj9a_"]}]}
